==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|ang|burg}}

===Etymology===
{{proto|Germanic|burgs|lang=ang}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|bʰərgʰ-|fortified elevation|lang=ang}}.  Cognate with Old Frisian ''[[burch]]'', Old Saxon ''[[burg]]'' (Dutch ''[[burcht]]''), Old High German ''[[burg]]'' (German ''[[Burg]]'' ‘castle’), Old Norse ''[[borg]]'' (Swedish ''[[borg]]''), Gothic [[𐌱𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌲𐍃]] ‘town’.  The Indo-European root is also the source of Celtic ''*brig-'' (Middle Irish ''brī'' ‘hill’, Welsh ''[[bera]]'' ‘stack’), Avestan ''brzant-'' ‘high’, Ancient Greek {{term|lang=grc|πύργος}} (''purgos'') ‘tower’, and Sanskrit ''-pur'' ‘city’.

